1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,760
I'm on the Energy and Commerce Committee.

2
00:00:01,760 --> 00:00:06,560
I love how the media all of a sudden thinks that AI is a topic that we should be talking

3
00:00:06,560 --> 00:00:07,560
about.

4
00:00:07,560 --> 00:00:10,660
I've been talking about this in Washington, D.C. since I came to Congress.

5
00:00:10,660 --> 00:00:15,000
The committee I serve on got the jurisdiction over AI.

6
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:20,960
We have a bipartisan work product, a marker bill, that was put out after hundreds and

7
00:00:20,960 --> 00:00:26,560
hundreds of hours of work by my friend Jay Obernolte of California working directly with

8
00:00:26,560 --> 00:00:31,480
Congresswoman Lori Trahan of Massachusetts because we need a federal framework for AI.

9
00:00:31,480 --> 00:00:34,360
And it is something that Congress needs to do.

10
00:00:34,360 --> 00:00:36,480
It's got to be a lawmaking exercise.

11
00:00:36,480 --> 00:00:41,760
Our problem is Washington and the lawmaking process doesn't move as fast as the technology.

12
00:00:41,760 --> 00:00:44,960
And that's what you've seen with social media, and it's run amok.

13
00:00:44,960 --> 00:00:47,400
There need to be guardrails in place.

14
00:00:47,400 --> 00:00:51,940
What we cannot have is 50 states all doing it their own way.

15
00:00:51,940 --> 00:00:56,820
We will over-regulate ourselves to the point where we will become Europe and the innovation

16
00:00:56,820 --> 00:00:59,380
will not be here in the United States of America.

17
00:00:59,380 --> 00:01:04,620
I mean, one cryptic tweet and all of a sudden everyone's hair is on fire about AI.

18
00:01:04,620 --> 00:01:12,300
I work on AI in AI policy every day, but it's not something that the mainstream picks up.

